          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Do European and US bodies differ on when to start medication for stage 1 hypertension?",
          "answer": "Yes, there are differences between European and US guidelines regarding when to start medication for stage 1 hypertension. \n\nIn the United States, a diagnostic threshold for hypertension is set at 130/80 mm Hg. However, European guidelines start the classification of office hypertension at 140/90 mm Hg. This means that a reading between 130-139 / 80-89 mm Hg is viewed as high-normal blood pressure in Europe rather than stage 1 hypertension. As a result, the number of individuals classified as hypertensive can vary significantly depending on which guideline is applied (source: guideline-hypertension-diagnosis-and-bp-targets).\n\nWhen considering treatment, US guidelines recommend starting medication in individuals with stage 1 hypertension who have established cardiovascular disease, diabetes, chronic kidney disease, or a 10-year cardiovascular risk at or above 10%. For those without these conditions, lifestyle measures are advised first, with reassessment in three to six months before considering medications (source: guideline-hypertension-first-line-treatment).\n\nI could not cover specific medication options or detailed differences in treatment recommendations.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
